-- Q1: medium / L2 — Discontinued operations EPS presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Millbrook Enterprises reports income from continuing operations of $720,000 and a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $180,000, resulting in net income of $540,000. The company has 120,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding and no preferred stock. What is earnings per share for discontinued operations?', '["$6.00", "$4.50", "($1.50)", "$1.50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities must present EPS for discontinued operations either on the face of the income statement or in the notes. The discontinued operations EPS equals the net-of-tax loss ($180,000) divided by weighted-average shares (120,000), yielding ($1.50). Choice B ($4.50) incorrectly uses net income rather than the discontinued operations component, ignoring the requirement to compute EPS separately for each income statement line item.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — Two-class method for participating preferred stock
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Grandview Holdings has net income of $900,000 with 200,000 common shares and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock outstanding for all of 2025. The preferred carries a $2.00 per-share annual dividend and participates equally with common in additional distributions. Total declared dividends are $100,000 for preferred and $400,000 for common. Analyze how the participation feature affects basic EPS compared to a standard preferred dividend deduction.', '["It has no effect because participating rights are only relevant for diluted EPS", "It reduces basic EPS because undistributed earnings must be allocated between common and participating preferred shares", "It increases basic EPS because the preferred dividends are not deducted from net income", "It only affects basic EPS if dividends are actually declared in excess of the stated rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, the two-class method requires undistributed earnings to be allocated between common and participating preferred shares based on their respective rights to share in earnings. This reduces the numerator for basic EPS compared to simply deducting the stated preferred dividend. Choice D is incorrect because the two-class method applies based on the contractual right to participate, regardless of whether excess dividends are actually declared.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L2 — Weighted-average shares with mid-year issuance for stock compensation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redstone Corp. has 80,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2026. On July 1, 2026, the company issues 20,000 shares to employees upon vesting of restricted stock awards. Net income for 2026 is $450,000 with no preferred stock. What is basic EPS?', '["$5.63", "$4.50", "$4.00", "$5.00"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued during the period are included in the weighted-average from the date issued. The 20,000 shares issued on July 1 are weighted for 6 months (6/12), yielding a weighted-average of 90,000 shares (80,000 + 10,000). Basic EPS is $450,000 / 90,000 = $5.00. Choice B ($4.50) incorrectly includes the 20,000 shares for the full year rather than weighting them from the issuance date.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["Public entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock", "All entities that prepare GAAP financial statements", "Only entities listed on a national stock exchange", "Only entities with total assets exceeding $10 million"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock (such as options, warrants, or convertible securities). Private companies are not required to present EPS, though they may voluntarily do so. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 specifically limits the requirement to public entities, not all GAAP-reporting entities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Retroactive restatement for stock dividend after year-end
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Industries had 100,000 common shares outstanding during all of 2025 and reported basic EPS of $4.20 based on net income of $420,000. On February 15, 2026, before the 2025 financial statements were issued, Crestview declared a 20% stock dividend. What basic EPS figure should Crestview report for 2025?', '["$4.20", "$3.50", "$5.04", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance must be given retroactive recognition in computing EPS. The 20% stock dividend increases weighted-average shares to 120,000 (100,000 x 1.20), producing restated EPS of $3.50 ($420,000 / 120,000). Choice A ($4.20) incorrectly ignores the retroactive restatement required for post-period stock dividends.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Actual conversion of preferred stock mid-year effect on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornfield Group has net income of $1,200,000 and 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2026. On July 1, 2026, holders convert 5,000 shares of $100 par, 8% convertible preferred stock into 25,000 common shares. Analyze how the mid-year conversion affects the numerator and denominator in the basic EPS calculation.', '["Both numerator and denominator are unaffected because conversion only impacts diluted EPS", "The numerator deducts the full year of preferred dividends, and the denominator includes the converted shares weighted from July 1", "The numerator deducts only the pre-conversion preferred dividends, and the denominator includes no converted shares until the following year", "The numerator deducts preferred dividends only for the pre-conversion period, and the denominator includes the converted shares weighted from July 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10 and 45-11, when convertible preferred stock is actually converted, basic EPS deducts preferred dividends only for the pre-conversion period (January through June, or $20,000) and includes the 25,000 converted common shares weighted from the conversion date (6/12 = 12,500 incremental weighted shares). Choice B is incorrect because it deducts the full year of preferred dividends ($40,000) despite conversion occurring mid-year, overstating the deduction.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS with convertible preferred using if-converted method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Corp. reports net income of $920,000 with 200,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding. The company has 10,000 shares of $100 par, 5% cumulative convertible preferred stock, each convertible into 4 common shares. Using the if-converted method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$3.83", "$4.35", "$3.63", "$3.68"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock adds back preferred dividends ($50,000) to the numerator and includes potential common shares (10,000 x 4 = 40,000) in the denominator. Diluted EPS = $920,000 / 240,000 = $3.83. Since $3.83 is less than basic EPS of $4.35, the conversion is dilutive. Choice C ($3.63) incorrectly fails to add back the preferred dividends to the numerator when adding the converted shares to the denominator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Evaluate sequential dilution test using control number
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fairmont Industries reports income from continuing operations of $600,000, a gain on discontinued operations (net of tax) of $150,000, and net income of $750,000, with 150,000 weighted-average common shares. After including stock options (8,000 incremental shares), diluted EPS from continuing operations becomes $3.80. Including convertible bonds next changes the result to $3.82. Evaluate whether the convertible bonds should be included in the diluted EPS computation.', '["Yes, because diluted EPS from continuing operations of $3.82 is still below basic EPS of $4.00", "Yes, because net income-based diluted EPS decreases when the bonds are included", "No, because each security must be tested individually against basic EPS, not incrementally", "No, because including the bonds increased diluted EPS from $3.80 to $3.82, making them antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are included in diluted EPS one at a time in order of their dilutive effect. A security is antidilutive if its inclusion increases diluted EPS from continuing operations. Here, adding the convertible bonds raised diluted EPS from $3.80 to $3.82, so they are antidilutive at this step and must be excluded. Choice A incorrectly focuses on the comparison to basic EPS rather than the incremental effect at each sequential step.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — RSUs under treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Technologies has 500,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $2,000,000. The company has 30,000 unvested restricted stock units (RSUs) with $12.00 of unrecognized compensation cost per unit. The average market price during the year is $40.00 per share. Using the treasury stock method under ASC 260, how many incremental shares do the RSUs add to diluted EPS?', '["30,000", "21,000", "9,000", "18,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, RSUs are treated under the treasury stock method using unrecognized compensation cost as the assumed proceeds. The 30,000 RSUs generate assumed proceeds of $360,000 (30,000 x $12.00), which hypothetically repurchases 9,000 shares ($360,000 / $40.00). The incremental shares are 30,000 minus 9,000 = 21,000. Choice A (30,000) incorrectly includes all RSU shares without applying the treasury stock method repurchase assumption.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Treasury stock repurchase effect on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearwater Resources had 400,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2026. On September 1, 2026, the company repurchased 60,000 shares as treasury stock. Net income for 2026 was $1,400,000 with no preferred stock. Analyze how the September 1 repurchase affects Clearwater''s basic EPS compared to having no repurchase.', '["Basic EPS increases because the repurchase reduces the weighted-average shares in the denominator", "Basic EPS decreases because treasury stock repurchases reduce net income", "Basic EPS is unaffected because treasury shares are excluded only from diluted EPS", "Basic EPS is unaffected because repurchased shares remain in the weighted-average for the full year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury stock repurchases reduce the weighted-average common shares from the repurchase date. Clearwater''s weighted-average decreases from 400,000 to 380,000 (400,000 x 8/12 + 340,000 x 4/12), increasing basic EPS from $3.50 to approximately $3.68 because the same net income is spread over fewer shares. Choice D incorrectly suggests repurchased shares remain in the weighted-average, ignoring the reduction from the repurchase date.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Complex capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what characterizes a complex capital structure?', '["An entity with more than one class of common stock outstanding", "An entity with total debt exceeding total equity", "An entity with securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS", "An entity with more than 1,000 shareholders of record"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS, such as convertible bonds, convertible preferred stock, stock options, or warrants. Entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because multiple classes of common stock alone do not create a complex capital structure unless there are potentially dilutive securities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Control number for antidilution test
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the ''control number'' used to determine whether potential common shares are dilutive or antidilutive?', '["Net income attributable to common shareholders", "Income from continuing operations attributable to common shareholders", "Comprehensive income attributable to common shareholders", "Operating income before interest and taxes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations (adjusted for preferred dividends) is the control number for determining whether potential common shares are dilutive. If including a security would increase EPS from continuing operations (or decrease the loss per share), the security is antidilutive and must be excluded. Choice A is incorrect because net income includes discontinued operations, which is not used as the control number.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Mid-year option grant: which average market price to use
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Systems granted 24,000 stock options to executives on July 1, 2026, with an exercise price of $30. The average market price for the full year was $45, and the average market price from July 1 to December 31 was $50. Analyze which average market price is appropriate for the treasury stock method and the resulting incremental shares added to the denominator.', '["The full-year average of $45, resulting in 8,000 incremental shares for the full year", "The full-year average of $45, resulting in 4,000 incremental shares weighted for six months", "The July-December average of $50, resulting in 9,600 incremental shares for the full year", "The July-December average of $50, resulting in 4,800 incremental shares weighted for six months"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, options outstanding for only part of the period are included on a time-weighted basis using the average market price for the period the options are outstanding. Since the options were granted July 1, the July-December average of $50 is appropriate, yielding 9,600 incremental shares (24,000 minus 14,400) weighted for six months (4,800 shares). Choice A incorrectly uses the full-year average and fails to time-weight the incremental shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS with convertible bonds (interest add-back net of tax)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Industries reports net income of $1,080,000 and has 250,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has $1,000,000 face value of 6% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond convertible into 20 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. Using the if-converted method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$4.17", "$4.32", "$4.00", "$4.22"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible bonds adds back the after-tax interest to the numerator and includes potential common shares in the denominator. The numerator increases by $45,000 ($60,000 interest x (1 minus 0.25 tax rate)), and 20,000 potential shares are added. Diluted EPS = $1,125,000 / 270,000 = $4.17. Choice D ($4.22) incorrectly adds back the full $60,000 interest without the tax adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Evaluate weighted-average shares for interim EPS computation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Belmont Corporation reports results for the nine months ended September 30, 2026. It had 200,000 shares outstanding January 1 through June 30 and 240,000 shares from July 1 through September 30 after a public offering. The controller computed year-to-date EPS using 240,000 shares (the period-end balance). Evaluate whether this approach is correct.', '["Correct, because interim EPS uses the share count at the end of the interim period", "Correct, because shares issued during the year are included for the full year-to-date period", "Incorrect, because the year-to-date weighted-average should be approximately 213,333 shares", "Incorrect, because interim EPS should use the projected full-year weighted-average of 220,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average number of shares for year-to-date interim EPS must reflect the actual time-weighting over the reporting period. The nine-month weighted-average is 213,333 (200,000 x 6/9 + 240,000 x 3/9), not the period-end balance of 240,000. Choice A is incorrect because using the period-end share count ignores the time-weighting principle, which would understate EPS by overstating the denominator.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Where EPS must be presented
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, where must an entity with a complex capital structure present earnings per share data?', '["Only in the notes to the financial statements", "In the management discussion and analysis section", "Only on the statement of cash flows", "On the face of the income statement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and net income. EPS for discontinued operations may be presented on the income statement or in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires presentation on the face of the income statement, not merely in the notes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred shares and EPS treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Pharmaceuticals issued 50,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable convertible preferred stock with a $100 par value and a 7% annual dividend. Under ASC 480, these shares are classified as liabilities, and the related dividends are treated as interest expense. The tax rate is 25%. Evaluate how the liability classification affects the diluted EPS computation under the if-converted method.', '["Dividends are deducted from net income in computing basic EPS, and the if-converted method ignores the liability classification", "Dividends paid on the preferred stock are treated as interest expense and added back to the numerator on an after-tax basis for the if-converted dilutive impact", "It has no effect because mandatorily redeemable shares are always excluded from EPS", "The shares are excluded from diluted EPS because liability-classified instruments cannot generate potential common shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40 and ASC 480-10-25, when mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability, its dividends are reported as interest expense. For diluted EPS under the if-converted method, this interest expense is added back to the numerator on an after-tax basis (similar to convertible bonds), and potential common shares are added to the denominator. Choice D is incorrect because liability classification does not prevent the instrument from generating potential common shares if conversion features exist.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Weighted-average shares with repurchase and reissuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stratton Corp. had 350,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2026. On May 1, the company repurchased 50,000 shares as treasury stock. On November 1, the company reissued 20,000 of those treasury shares. What is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding for 2026?', '["350,000", "300,000", "320,000", "316,667"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury stock transactions are reflected in the weighted-average from their respective dates. Stratton''s weighted-average is: (350,000 x 4/12) + (300,000 x 6/12) + (320,000 x 2/12) = 116,667 + 150,000 + 53,333 = 320,000 shares. Choice D (316,667) incorrectly ignores the November 1 treasury stock reissuance, treating the 300,000 share balance as continuing through year-end.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Antidilutive securities exclusion using per-share impact ranking
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Heritage Financial reports basic EPS of $5.00 based on net income of $750,000 and 150,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has stock options producing 6,000 incremental shares with no numerator impact, and convertible bonds adding $50,000 after-tax interest and 8,000 potential shares. After including the options (diluted EPS = $4.81), would the convertible bonds be included in diluted EPS?', '["No, because the bonds'' per-share impact of $6.25 exceeds the options-adjusted diluted EPS of $4.81, making the bonds antidilutive at this step", "Yes, because diluted EPS must include all convertible securities regardless of their per-share impact", "Yes, because the bonds'' per-share impact of $3.13 decreases diluted EPS further", "No, but only because the bonds'' per-share impact exceeds basic EPS of $5.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities are ranked by their per-share impact ($50,000 / 8,000 = $6.25 for the bonds) and tested sequentially. After including the options (diluted EPS = $4.81), adding the bonds would increase diluted EPS to $4.88 (($750,000 + $50,000) / 164,000), making them antidilutive. The bonds must be excluded. Choice B is incorrect because antidilutive securities are never included in diluted EPS regardless of their convertible nature.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Line items requiring per-share presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, for which income statement line items must a public entity present per-share amounts?', '["Gross profit and operating income", "Revenue and cost of goods sold", "Operating income and comprehensive income", "Income from continuing operations and net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, public entities must present basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. Per-share amounts for discontinued operations may be presented on the income statement or in the notes. Choice C is incorrect because comprehensive income does not require per-share presentation under ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Shares issued in business combination weighted from acquisition date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarwood Holdings had 180,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2026. On April 1, 2026, Briarwood acquired Summit LLC by issuing 60,000 common shares. Net income for 2026 was $840,000 with no preferred stock. What is the weighted-average number of shares for computing basic EPS?', '["240,000", "225,000", "210,000", "180,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued as consideration in a business combination are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date. The 60,000 shares issued on April 1 are weighted for nine months (60,000 x 9/12 = 45,000), yielding 225,000 total weighted-average shares. Choice A (240,000) incorrectly includes the acquisition shares for the full year, but they can only be included from the date the acquirer obtains control.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Evaluate contingently issuable shares based on earnings targets
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hawthorne Corp. has an earnout agreement requiring issuance of 40,000 additional shares if cumulative earnings over 2025-2027 exceed $5,000,000. Through December 31, 2025, cumulative earnings are $1,500,000. Current-year 2025 earnings are $1,500,000. The controller included 40,000 contingent shares in diluted EPS because she projects total three-year earnings will exceed $5,000,000. Evaluate whether the controller''s treatment is correct.', '["Incorrect, because the extrapolation of current-period earnings ($1,500,000 per year x 3 = $4,500,000) does not meet the $5,000,000 threshold, so the shares are excluded", "Incorrect, because contingently issuable shares are never included in diluted EPS until the contingency is fully resolved", "Correct, because management projections are permissible for evaluating contingently issuable shares", "Correct, because contingent shares must be included whenever there is a reasonable possibility of issuance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares based on earnings targets are included in diluted EPS only if current-period earnings, extrapolated over the remaining contingency period, would satisfy the condition. With $1,500,000 annual earnings projected over three years ($4,500,000 total), the $5,000,000 target is not met, so the 40,000 shares are excluded. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 does not permit management projections — the test is mechanical, using current-period earnings as the assumed constant.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Effect of share repurchase on EPS components
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prescott Manufacturing has 500,000 shares outstanding, net income of $2,500,000, and basic EPS of $5.00. The board approves a $4,000,000 share repurchase at $50 per share (80,000 shares) effective January 1. Assuming net income remains unchanged, analyze the effect on basic EPS.', '["Basic EPS decreases to $4.52 because the cash outflow for the repurchase reduces net income", "Basic EPS is unchanged because the repurchase is a treasury stock transaction with no income statement effect on the numerator", "Basic EPS increases to approximately $5.95 because fewer shares remain in the denominator while net income is unaffected", "Basic EPS increases only if the repurchase is funded entirely from retained earnings"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, share repurchases reduce the weighted-average shares in the EPS denominator. With 420,000 shares after the repurchase and unchanged net income of $2,500,000, basic EPS increases to approximately $5.95. The repurchase reduces equity but does not affect net income. Choice B correctly notes that the repurchase has no income statement effect, but incorrectly concludes EPS is unchanged — EPS changes because the denominator decreases.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average shares outstanding
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does the term ''weighted-average common shares outstanding'' mean in the context of EPS computation?', '["The number of shares outstanding adjusted for the time period each share was outstanding during the reporting period", "The total number of shares authorized in the corporate charter", "The number of shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period", "The average of the beginning and ending share balances"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average common shares outstanding represents the number of shares adjusted for the portion of the reporting period each was outstanding. Shares issued mid-year are weighted by the fraction of the year they were outstanding. Choice D is incorrect because simply averaging beginning and ending balances does not properly account for the timing of individual share transactions during the period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Retroactive treatment of stock split on prior-period EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxglove Corp. reported basic EPS of $6.40 for 2025 based on net income of $640,000 and 100,000 weighted-average shares. In March 2026, before issuing its comparative financial statements, Foxglove executes a 2-for-1 stock split. Analyze how the stock split affects the presentation of 2025 EPS in the comparative statements.', '["2025 EPS is restated to $4.27 by dividing the original EPS by 1.5", "2025 EPS remains at $6.40 with a footnote disclosing the subsequent stock split", "2025 EPS is retroactively restated to $3.20 by doubling the weighted-average shares", "2025 EPS remains at $6.40 because the split occurred in a different fiscal year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance must be retroactively reflected in all EPS computations presented. The 2-for-1 split doubles the 2025 weighted-average shares to 200,000, restating basic EPS to $3.20. Choice B is incorrect because a mere footnote disclosure is insufficient — the standard requires retroactive restatement of the per-share amounts to ensure comparability.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Two-class method allocation of undistributed earnings
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Silverstone Holdings has net income of $800,000, 200,000 common shares, and 25,000 shares of Series A preferred stock with a $3.00 annual dividend that participates equally with common in distributions beyond the stated rate. Declared dividends total $175,000 ($75,000 to preferred, $100,000 to common). Analyze how the remaining $625,000 of undistributed earnings are allocated under the two-class method.', '["All $625,000 is allocated to common shareholders after deducting the stated preferred dividend", "The $625,000 is allocated approximately $555,556 to common and $69,444 to preferred based on their relative outstanding shares", "The $625,000 is allocated 50/50 between common and preferred because they participate equally", "Undistributed earnings are excluded from the EPS computation entirely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings to each class based on their respective rights to share in earnings. Since the preferred participates equally after its stated dividend, undistributed earnings of $625,000 are allocated by relative shares: common receives $555,556 (200,000/225,000) and preferred receives $69,444 (25,000/225,000). Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the preferred stock''s contractual participation right in undistributed earnings.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Entity NOT required to present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Which of the following entities is NOT required to present earnings per share under ASC 260?', '["A publicly traded corporation listed on the NYSE", "A company with stock options traded over the counter", "A privately held limited liability company", "A publicly traded entity with convertible bonds outstanding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock. A privately held LLC has no publicly traded equity instruments and is therefore exempt from the EPS presentation requirement. Choice A is incorrect because NYSE-listed corporations are public entities that must comply with ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Multiple option series with different exercise prices
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartfield Corp. has two sets of stock options: (1) 15,000 options with an exercise price of $25 and (2) 10,000 options with an exercise price of $45. The average market price for the year is $40. Analyze the combined effect of both option sets on diluted EPS.', '["Only Set 1 adds 5,625 incremental shares because Set 2 is antidilutive with an exercise price above the average market price", "Both sets are excluded because stock options must be evaluated as a single combined pool", "Both sets together add 25,000 incremental shares since all outstanding options must be included", "Only Set 2 is included because higher exercise prices produce greater per-share dilution"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, stock options are dilutive only when the exercise price is below the average market price. Set 1 ($25 exercise price) is in-the-money and adds 5,625 incremental shares under the treasury stock method (15,000 minus 15,000 x $25/$40). Set 2 ($45 exercise price) exceeds the $40 average market price, making it antidilutive and excluded. Choice C is incorrect because each option series is evaluated independently, and antidilutive options must be excluded.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Net loss from continuing operations makes all securities antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Blackstone Dynamics reports a net loss from continuing operations of $400,000 and income from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $600,000, resulting in net income of $200,000. The company has 100,000 weighted-average shares and stock options producing 3,000 incremental shares under the treasury stock method. The controller plans to include the options in diluted EPS because net income is positive. Evaluate whether the controller''s approach is correct.', '["Correct, because net income is positive and the options are mathematically dilutive to net income per share", "Correct, because diluted EPS is computed only when net income is positive, regardless of income composition", "Incorrect, because options are always excluded from diluted EPS when the entity reports any discontinued operations", "Incorrect, because the control number is the loss from continuing operations, and including the options would reduce the loss per share, making them antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, the control number for the antidilution test is income (loss) from continuing operations, not net income. Because Blackstone has a loss from continuing operations of $400,000, including the 3,000 incremental shares would reduce the loss per share (from $4.00 to $3.88), which is antidilutive. All potentially dilutive securities are therefore excluded. Choice A is incorrect because it uses net income as the dilution test, ignoring the requirement to use continuing operations as the control number.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does ''antidilutive'' mean in the context of earnings per share?', '["A security that would decrease earnings per share or increase loss per share if included in diluted EPS", "A security that would increase earnings per share or decrease loss per share if included in diluted EPS", "A security that has no effect on either basic or diluted EPS", "A security that reduces the number of shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, an antidilutive security is one whose inclusion in the diluted EPS computation would increase EPS or decrease loss per share compared to basic EPS. Antidilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS because their inclusion would make the metric appear more favorable to common shareholders. Choice A describes a dilutive security, which is the opposite — one whose inclusion decreases EPS or increases loss per share.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
